---
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import { FeedbackLoopCycle } from "../components/FeedbackLoopCycle";
import PageShell from "../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Feedback Loops - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="feedback-loops">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Every interaction with an AI tool can end up in a feedback loop. This can be both a good and a bad thing.
			</p>

			<FeedbackLoopCycle client:visible />

			<h3>Fix Loop:Be Willing to Start Over</h3>

			<p>
				AI-generated output is cheap. Your time is not. When you find yourself on the third round of asking the AI to fix
				its own output, you are almost certainly spending more time than it would take to revert everything, rethink the
				prompt, and start a fresh conversation.
			</p>

			<p>
				This feels wrong at first. You have three rounds of context in the conversation, the AI "knows" what you want by now,
				and throwing that away feels wasteful. But what the AI actually has is three rounds of confused, contradictory context.
				The original intent is buried. Each fix layered on top of the last makes the next fix harder.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Before starting over, take 30 seconds to write down what went wrong. Not in the AI chat, just for yourself. "It used
					a class component instead of a hook" or "It didn't account for the null case in the API response." That note becomes
					the missing context for your next prompt.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Read Before You React</h3>

			<p>
				When AI output doesn't match what you expected, the instinct is to immediately ask for a fix. Resist that for a moment.
				Read what the AI actually produced, read through its reasoning. Look at the assumptions it made. Often the output isn't wrong so much as it
				reveals a gap between what you said and what you meant.
			</p>

			<p>
				Maybe you asked for "form validation" but didn't mention that you already have a Zod schema on the backend. The AI
				wrote validation from scratch because it didn't know. Take your original prompt, and add the missing information, and start over.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="example" title="Questions to ask yourself">
				<ul class="space-y-2 m-0 list-none pl-0">
					<li><strong>What did it assume?</strong> Look for patterns, libraries, or approaches it chose without being told.</li>
					<li><strong>What did I leave out?</strong> Constraints, existing code, naming conventions, or dependencies it couldn't know about.</li>
					<li><strong>What did it get right?</strong> The parts that worked tell you what your prompt communicated effectively.</li>
				</ul>
			</Callout>

			<h3>The Cost of Continuing vs. Restarting</h3>

			<p>
				There is a tipping point in every AI conversation where continuing is slower than restarting. It usually comes
				around the second or third correction. At that point, the conversation context contains:
			</p>

			<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
				<li>The original (incomplete) prompt</li>
				<li>The first (wrong) output</li>
				<li>Your correction</li>
				<li>A second attempt that partially addressed the correction</li>
				<li>Another correction about the parts it missed</li>
			</ul>

			<p>
				The AI is now trying to reconcile all of that. A fresh conversation with one well-written prompt that includes the
				context you've now identified as important will almost always produce a better result, faster.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-2">The feedback loop workflow</div>
					<ol class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5 m-0">
						<li>Write a prompt with as much relevant context as you can</li>
						<li>Read the output carefully, not just whether it "works"</li>
						<li>If it's close: make targeted adjustments in the same conversation</li>
						<li>If it's off: note what was missing, use Git to revert the changes, start a new conversation with a better prompt</li>
						<li>Commit what works, then move to the next task</li>
					</ol>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3>This Gets Easier</h3>

			<p>
				As you spend time in this loop you will find that you will get better at identifying what the AI needs to know, your first prompts
				get more complete, and you spend less time in correction cycles. You start to develop an instinct for what to include:
				which files to reference, what constraints to mention, which existing patterns to point to.
			</p>

			<p>
				This skill will need to evolve over time as well, different tools, different models, different contexts, different projects will each require a different approach. 
				As you build this muscle memory you will find that you will be able to identify the issues faster and resolve them faster.
			</p>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
